# Stalkerware, mSpy-style (2010 onwards). Phone monitoring apps used to watch a partner. This scene shows only what the person being watched can notice and where to get help.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee is a front-desk coordinator on a lunch break. Dana confirms one real sign-in alert safely, then meets one she did not cause.
# The app lives on a phone, so this desktop shows what the victim could really see from a computer: the cloud account's alert and device list, and the phone's battery and data page.
# The victim in this story is invented. The scene never shows how to install or hide anything, and it ends with where to get safe help.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | Stalkerware is a phone app that secretly copies someone's messages, calls, photos and location to another person. Sold as a "family monitor" (mSpy and its many look-alikes), it is often used by an abuser against a partner.
  | Some of these tools work through an app on the phone; others pull the cloud backup with the account password. This scene shows both.
  | It is installed by hand, in a few minutes, by someone who has the phone and its passcode, or who knows the account password. It hides its icon and keeps a name like "Sync Service". The person being watched sees nothing on screen.
  | This scene is about noticing the signs and getting help, never about installing it. The topic can be upsetting: the help page is the last task, and the person in the story is invented.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, front-desk coordinator at Northwind Traders. At lunch you use the office computer for your own errands, including your own email: it is the one screen nobody at home can see.
  | Lately someone at home has known things they should not: where you had lunch, who messaged you. You have not worked out how.
  | You have done 2 of today's 4 lunch-break chores. Two to go: the alert about your new tablet, and whatever else comes up.
  | Your cloud sign-in is in a text file on the desktop, next to your phone passcode. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 2 4 Lunch-break chores done
spotted signs someone was watching

# Dana's desk (soft flags: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/cloud-login.txt
  at 300 520
  | Cloud Service account (phone backup)
  | Account: dana.okafor@mail-box.example
  | Password: Maple-Orchard-52!
file Desktop/phone-passcode.txt
  at 460 520
  | Dana's phone (training copy)
  | Passcode: 2580
  | Recovery question (first street): Alder Lane

# The cloud service's own pages: a sign-in page, and the device list behind it.
page cloud-login account.cloud-service.example/sign-in
  title Cloud Service: sign in
  brand Cloud Service
  favorite
  heading Sign in to your account
  | Welcome back. Sign in to see your devices and backups.
  field Account = dana.okafor@mail-box.example
  field secret Password = Maple-Orchard-52!
  button Sign in
page tablet-ok account.cloud-service.example/devices/confirmed
  title Device confirmed
  brand Cloud Service
  heading Thanks, device confirmed
  | Dana's tablet stays signed in to your account.
page devices-ok account.cloud-service.example/devices/tablet
  title Your account: devices
  brand Cloud Service
  requires cloud-login
  heading Devices signed in to your account
  | A new device signed in today. Check that you know it.
  section Signed-in devices
  columns Device | Last active | Where
  cells Dana's phone | Today | Home
  cells Dana's tablet (new) | This morning, 8:12 | Home
  note The tablet signed in once, from your home address, when you set it up.
  button Yes, this was me -> tablet-ok
mail tablet-alert
  from Cloud Service <no-reply@cloud-service.example>
  to dana.okafor@mail-box.example
  subject New sign-in: Dana's tablet
  sent 2026-11-02 08:13
  link devices-ok
  | Hello Dana,
  |
  | Your new tablet signed in to your account this morning.
  |
  | If this was you, no action is needed. You can review your devices at any time.

step Chore 3 of 4
  look Mail: the cloud service's message about your new tablet
  | You set up a new tablet this morning, so the cloud service sends an alert. This one is about you. Confirm it the normal way, so you know what a normal alert looks like.
notify mail Cloud Service
  | New sign-in: Dana's tablet

step The link goes to your account
  look the link under the message
  when mail tablet-alert
  | The email links to the service's own address, account.cloud-service.example. Hover over a link before you click it, to check where it goes.
open tablet-alert

step Sign in as usual
  look the two boxes: Account and Password
  when visit devices-ok
  move Copy the details from cloud-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The account asks you to sign in first. Two boxes: Account and Password. Remember what this page normally looks like.
open devices-ok

step Check, then confirm
  look the list: your phone and your new tablet, both at home
  when choose cloud-login 1
  move Read the list of devices, then click “Yes, this was me”.
  | You are in. The list has two devices, and you know both: your phone, and the tablet you set up this morning at home.
signin cloud-login

step 3 of 4 done
  look the counter in the guide: one chore left
  when choose devices-ok any
  | Confirmed. One chore to go. And another alert has just arrived.
choose devices-ok 1
quota +1

# The lure: a second alert, from the same service, about a device Dana did not add.
page helpline safer-phone-help.example/helpline
  title Safer phone help: helplines
  brand Safer Phone Help
  heading A helpline can make a plan with you
  | You do not have to decide alone, or tonight.
  section Helpline (fictional number)
  row Call = 555 0123
  row Open = every hour of every day
  row Tip = call from a phone the other person cannot see or check
  section Before you call
  item Write down what you noticed, with dates, and keep it somewhere they cannot see.
  item Ask what to do about the phone before you touch it: the helpline will plan that with you.
  item If you are in danger now, call your local emergency number.
# weather is defined twice: the help page's Quick exit opens it, and its button goes back to help once help is defined.
page weather daily-weather.example/today
  title Weather today
  heading Weather today
page help safer-phone-help.example
  title Safer phone help
  brand Safer Phone Help
  heading If you think someone is watching your phone
  | You are not to blame, and help is available. If you are in danger now, call your local emergency number.
  section First steps
  item Use a device the other person cannot reach to look for help.
  item Do not delete anything yet: removing it may alert them.
  item Contact a domestic-abuse helpline or someone you trust.
  item Save evidence such as screenshots and dates, somewhere they cannot see.
  item With a helper, change passwords from the safe device, then check the phone.
  section Leaving quickly
  note Quick exit leaves this site at once. In this training copy it opens a neutral weather page.
  button Find a helpline -> helpline
  button Quick exit -> weather
page weather daily-weather.example/today
  title Weather today
  brand Daily Weather
  heading Weather today
  | Sunny spells, 14 degrees. This neutral page is where Quick exit goes in this training copy.
  button Back to safe help -> help
page removed account.cloud-service.example/devices/removed
  title Device removed
  brand Cloud Service
  heading Device removed
  | The unknown device is signed out of your account.
  section What happens next
  tone warn
  note The app on your phone is still there. Whoever set it up may see that your account stopped answering, and may not know why.
  button Get safe help first -> help
page phone-usage account.cloud-service.example/devices/phone
  title Cloud Service: phone battery and data
  brand Cloud Service
  requires cloud-login
  heading Your phone: battery and data
  | Your phone sends this report to your account every week. It covers the last 7 days.
  section Battery use
  row Messages = 6%
  row Maps = 9%
  row Sync Service = 34%
  section Mobile data uploaded
  row Browser = 85 MB
  row Messages = 40 MB
  row Sync Service = 2.1 GB
  section Apps with special access
  tone warn
  row Sync Service = location always, photos, accessibility
  row Icon = hidden from the home screen
  row Installed = 2 days ago
  button Get safe help first -> help
  button Remove the app now -> removed
page devices account.cloud-service.example/devices
  title Your account: devices
  brand Cloud Service
  requires cloud-login
  heading Devices signed in to your account
  | These devices have used your account recently.
  section Signed-in devices
  columns Device | Last active | Where
  cells Dana's phone | Today | Home
  cells Dana's tablet (new) | This morning | Home
  cells Unknown device (Linux) | 2 minutes ago | Not home: 203.0.113.44
  section What this device did
  tone warn
  row Backup restored = 2 days ago
  row Photos viewed = 2 days ago
  row Location checked = every 15 minutes
  note If you did not add a device, someone may have your password or phone passcode.
  button See what my phone reports -> phone-usage
  button Remove this device -> removed
mail alert
  from Cloud Service <no-reply@cloud-service.example>
  to dana.okafor@mail-box.example
  subject New device signed in to your account
  sent 2026-11-02 12:31
  link devices
  | Hello Dana,
  |
  | A new device signed in to your account from 203.0.113.44.
  |
  | If this was you, no action is needed. If not, review your devices.
notify mail Cloud Service
  | New device signed in to your account

step A second alert
  look the message: a new device, and an address you do not know
  when mail alert
  | Same service, same kind of message, but this one is not about the tablet. A device you did not add has signed in, from 203.0.113.44. The alert is real, and an alert you do not recognise is a warning sign. Flag anything that looks wrong.
open alert

step The account lists a device you never added
  look the “Unknown device” row, and the activity beneath it
  when visit devices
  move Read the list and what the unknown device did, then press “See what my phone reports”.
  cue choice:devices-1 choice:removed-1 choice:help-1 choice:weather-1
  | You are still signed in, so the list opens at once. Two devices are yours; the third is not: Linux, 203.0.113.44, active 2 minutes ago, while you are at work.
  | It restored a backup and looked at photos two days ago, and it checks your location every 15 minutes. Someone with your password or your phone for a few minutes can set this up.
open devices

step What the phone itself shows
  look the “Sync Service” rows in battery, data and special access
  when choose devices 1
  move Read the three sections. Do not press “Remove the app now” yet: press “Get safe help first”.
  cue choice:phone-usage-1 choice:removed-1 choice:help-1 choice:weather-1
  | The report from your phone names an app you never installed. Sync Service uses a third of the battery, uploads 2.1 GB, and has access to your location, photos and accessibility.
  | Accessibility lets an app read everything on the screen. Sync Service was installed 2 days ago, the same day the unknown device restored your backup.
  | It has no icon, and monitoring apps hide like this. None of these signs proves it alone; together they are worth checking.
open phone-usage

step Do not delete anything yet
  look the help page: “Do not delete anything yet”
  when choose phone-usage 1
  move Read the first steps, then press “Find a helpline”.
  cue choice:help-1 choice:weather-1
  | The tempting move is “Remove the app now”. Removing stalkerware can alert the person who set it up, which can be dangerous.
  | The safest first step is to look for help from a device the other person cannot see, such as a friend's phone or a library computer, and to ask a trusted person or a helpline for a plan.
choose phone-usage 1

step 4 of 4 done
  look the helpline page and the counter in the guide
  when choose help 1
  | A person who knows how to help with this is a phone call away. Writing down what you noticed, and when, is the next thing you do. That chore was not on your list, and it was the important one.
choose help 1
quota +1

step What the app was passing on
  look the animation: what someone else could read, and from where
  | Two ways in: the hidden app on Dana's phone, or the cloud backup read with her account password. Both sent her messages, calls, photos and location to someone else.
  | Dana saw none of it. The signs were all outside the app: an alert, a device she never added, and a phone that was working too hard.
attack spyware-surveillance
  | account alert
  | unknown device
  | backup restored
  | messages copied
  | calls and location logged
  | photos viewed
  | read on someone else's screen
open devices

flag hard mail alert
  | The service's own alert about a device you did not add. An alert you do not recognise is a real warning sign.
flag hard section devices 1
  | A list with a device you never added: Linux, 203.0.113.44, active 2 minutes ago.
flag hard section devices 2
  | The unknown device restored a backup, viewed photos and checks your location every 15 minutes: watching, not signing in by mistake.
flag hard section phone-usage 1
  | An app you never open uses a third of the battery. A hidden app that always runs drains it.
flag hard section phone-usage 2
  | 2.1 GB uploaded by one app in a week, even with the screen off: the copies of your messages and photos on their way.
flag hard section phone-usage 3
  | An app with a bland name, no icon, and access to location, photos and accessibility, which lets it read everything on screen. It was installed the same day the unknown device restored your backup.
flag soft file Desktop/cloud-login.txt
  | A password in a text file: anyone who reaches this computer can read it. A password manager is safer.
flag soft file Desktop/phone-passcode.txt
  | A phone passcode and a recovery answer belong in no file at all. Someone who knows the passcode can install a monitoring app in minutes.

lesson
  | Stalkerware needs a few minutes with your phone or your account password. Watch for account alerts, unknown devices, a fast-draining battery and unexplained data use.
  | Do not delete anything yet. Removing it can alert the person who set it up. Get safe help first, from a device they cannot reach, and plan with a helpline or someone you trust.
  | Choose a passcode and password nobody else knows, turn on two-step sign-in, and check your devices list now and then.
